  <!-- EDUCATIONAL: COLOR SPACES FOR TEXTILES -->
  <section class="color-section color-section--border">
    <div class="color-container">
      <h2 class="section-title">Color Systems Used in Garment &amp; Textile Production</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">Different parts of the production chain use different color formats. Knowing which system to use — and when — prevents miscommunication between designers, buyers, and factories.</p>

      <div class="edu-grid">
        <div class="edu-card">
          <h3>HEX &amp; RGB — Digital Design</h3>
          <p>Used in design software (Adobe Illustrator, Photoshop, Figma) and digital communication. HEX is the web standard. RGB is the underlying model. These are additive systems — they mix light, not dye. They are starting points for color selection, not production standards.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="edu-card">
          <h3>Pantone TCX — Garment Manufacturing</h3>
          <p>The global standard for textile colors. Each TCX code corresponds to a color dyed onto a real cotton swatch. When you give a Pantone TCX code to a dye house, they can reproduce that color regardless of what software or screen you used. Always use TCX, not PMS, for fabric.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="edu-card">
          <h3>CIE LAB — Spectrophotometer Measurement</h3>
          <p>LAB is device-independent and represents color the way human eyes perceive it. Spectrophotometers in dye labs measure fabric in LAB values. Delta E calculations are done in LAB space. It is the most accurate system for comparing two colors mathematically, regardless of how or where they were created.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="edu-card">
          <h3>CMYK — Textile Printing &amp; Trims</h3>
          <p>Used for screen printing, heat transfer, and producing labels, patches, and woven trims. CMYK is subtractive — it describes ink on a surface. Note that CMYK values differ between paper and fabric due to different absorption rates. Always do a press proof on actual substrate before bulk production.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="edu-card">
          <h3>RAL — Hardware, Trims &amp; Accessories</h3>
          <p>RAL is a European standard mainly used for paints and coatings. In garment production, it is useful for specifying colors on metal zippers, buttons, rivets, buckles, and packaging where the material is not a textile. The converter finds the nearest RAL match for any input color.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="edu-card">
          <h3>NCS — European Textile Sourcing</h3>
          <p>Natural Color System is used by European designers and some Scandinavian mills to describe color in human-perceptual terms. If you source fabric from European suppliers, you may receive NCS codes alongside Pantone references. This converter calculates an approximate NCS notation from any color input.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top: 1rem;">Pantone TCX vs Pantone PMS — What Garment Manufacturers Need to Know</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">This is one of the most common and costly mistakes in garment color specification. Pantone PMS (Pantone Matching System) colors are designed for printing on coated and uncoated paper. They are mixed inks and sit on top of a surface. Pantone TCX colors are dyed into cotton fiber. The light absorption, reflectance, and visual result are fundamentally different between paper and fabric even when using the same Pantone number.</p>
      <p class="section-intro">A dye house that receives a PMS code has to make a judgment call about which dye formula to use, which introduces variation and requires extra lab dip rounds. A dye house that receives a TCX code works from an established dye formula built for that specific cotton swatch. The color approval process is faster and more accurate. When using this converter, the Pantone output always shows the nearest TCX match — the correct format for any textile production brief.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

