  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="color-section color-section--border">
    <div class="color-container">
      <h2 class="section-title">Color Converter FAQ — Textile &amp; Garment Color Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-container">

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is the difference between Pantone TCX and Pantone PMS?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Pantone PMS is designed for printing inks on paper. Pantone TCX (Textile Cotton eXtended) colors are dyed onto real cotton swatches, which is the correct reference for fabric dyeing. The same Pantone number in PMS and TCX can look visually similar on screen but produce different results in production because paper printing and fabric dyeing behave differently. For any garment production brief, always specify Pantone TCX codes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is the difference between Pantone TCX and TPX?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>TPX (Textile Paper eXtended) was the previous Pantone system for textiles, printed on paper cards. It has been discontinued and replaced by TCX, where colors are applied to actual cotton swatches. TCX is the current industry standard for all garment and textile color communication. If you have old TPX references, they should be updated to the nearest TCX equivalent before placing production orders.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Why is Delta E important in garment quality control?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Delta E gives a single number that describes how different two colors are. In garment manufacturing, it removes the subjectivity of visual color approval. A Delta E 2000 value below 1.0 means the colors are indistinguishable to the human eye. Values between 1 and 2 are acceptable for most branded garments. Above 5 is a visible mismatch that most buyers will reject. Specifying a Delta E tolerance in your purchase order — rather than relying on visual checks alone — reduces disputes between buyer and factory at the approval stage.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How do I convert HEX to Pantone TCX for garment production?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Enter your HEX code into the converter above. It will calculate the nearest Pantone TCX match from the full database and show the Delta E distance between your HEX color and that TCX color. A Delta E below 3 means the match is reasonably close. Always verify with a physical Pantone TCX swatch book before sending to production, as monitors cannot accurately represent how a dye will look on cotton.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How do I convert RGB to CMYK for textile printing?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Enter your RGB values into the converter. The CMYK result uses standard mathematical conversion through the subtractive color model. One important caveat: CMYK values for screen printing on fabric differ from offset printing on paper because fabric absorbs ink differently. The converter provides a mathematically accurate CMYK value, but you should always request a print sample on the actual fabric substrate before approving bulk production, as the output will vary by ink type, fabric construction, and printer settings.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How accurate are screen color representations for garments?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Screens display color in RGB light, which is additive. Fabric color comes from dye absorption, which is subtractive. Even a perfectly calibrated monitor cannot show exactly how a color will look on cotton, polyester, or blended fabric. Screen colors are a useful starting point for communicating intent, but they are not production standards. The only reliable production reference is a physical Pantone TCX swatch or an approved lab dip on the actual production fabric.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is the difference between Delta E 1976, 1994, and 2000?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Delta E 1976 is the original formula — straightforward but less accurate because human eyes are not equally sensitive to all colors. Delta E 1994 added weighting for hue and chroma to improve textile and printing accuracy. Delta E 2000 is the current industry standard and the most accurate, compensating for perceptual non-uniformity especially in blues and greens where the human eye sees differences more clearly. For garment quality control, always use Delta E 2000 when specifying tolerance limits with buyers.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is metamerism and why does it matter for garments?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Metamerism is when two colors look the same under one light source but differ under another. A garment that passes color approval in a factory under fluorescent light may look different from its reference swatch under D65 daylight in a store, or under the warm incandescent light in a fitting room. This causes customer complaints and returns. To catch metamerism before shipment, evaluate lab dips and production samples under at least three light sources: D65 daylight, TL84 fluorescent (store light), and tungsten or incandescent. Our Delta E calculator quantifies the difference numerically but cannot test for metamerism — that requires physical samples under multiple light sources.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How do I choose the right color space for garment production?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Use HEX and RGB for design files and digital communication. Use Pantone TCX for all fabric color references sent to dye houses and garment factories. Use CIE LAB for spectrophotometer-based quality control measurements. Use CMYK for printing applications including labels, heat transfers, screen printing, and packaging. Use RAL for accessories, trims, and hardware components. LCH, NCS, and Munsell are useful for technical documentation and color research but are less common in day-to-day production communication.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How can I ensure color consistency across production lots?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Use a physical Pantone TCX swatch as the color standard throughout the order — from initial lab dip through bulk production and final inspection. Specify a Delta E tolerance in writing before production starts, typically below 2.0 for branded garments. Measure all lab dips and production samples with a spectrophotometer and record the LAB values and Delta E against the standard. Keep dye lot records for reorders. Avoid relying on visual checks alone, especially across different factories or production runs, as human color perception varies with viewing conditions, fatigue, and individual differences.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is the NCS color system?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>NCS (Natural Color System) was developed in Sweden and is based on how human vision perceives color rather than on physical light or ink mixtures. An NCS code describes the blackness, chromaticness, and hue angle of a color. It is commonly used in Scandinavian and European design and textile industries. If you receive NCS references from European fabric suppliers or designers, this converter can help you translate them to Pantone TCX or LAB values for use with your factory.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is the Munsell color system used for in textiles?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Munsell organizes color by hue, value (lightness on a scale of 0 to 10), and chroma (color intensity from 0 outward). It is used in color research, scientific documentation, and by some Asian and European manufacturers for technical color grading. Munsell notation is also used in some soil science, archaeology, and forensics applications that occasionally intersect with natural dye research in textile production. This converter calculates an approximate Munsell notation from any input color.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is color fastness and why does it matter?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Color fastness measures how well a dye holds up to washing, rubbing, light exposure, and sweat. A garment can pass initial color approval but still fail if the dye bleeds in the first wash or fades after a few weeks in a store window. Color conversion and Delta E matching confirm that the initial color is correct — color fastness testing, done to ISO or AATCC standards, confirms that it will stay correct over the product's life. Both are needed for a complete quality process. Common tests include wash fastness (ISO 105-C06), light fastness (ISO 105-B02), and rubbing fastness (ISO 105-X12).</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is RAL and when is it used in garment production?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>RAL is a European color standard most widely used for industrial paints, coatings, and powder coatings. In garment production it is relevant for non-textile components that need to match a fabric color: metal zippers, alloy buttons, plastic buckles, painted hardware, hang tags, and retail packaging. If a jacket has a specific green fabric and the metal zipper puller needs to coordinate, the RAL match gives the hardware supplier a reference they can work with. This converter finds the nearest RAL Classic match from any color input.</p>
          </div>
